 "observations": [
  "The page number «140» stands at the head of the page, centred, with a rule below it; at the left the number of the table, «Tab. 29», in bold sans serif, underlined. The page has no signature. A small mark above the first title is a speck; not transcribed. The titles (L01-L03), in bold, the sigla in roman; L03, the place of Saturn's node, in smaller bold type, «3 S. 13 G. 12 M.» with «G.» and «M.» for the «Gr.» and «Min.» of pp. 138-139 (JP2 0170-0171), followed by the sigla «(CO)» in roman instead of a note. The folios of the manuscripts stand at the left of the last title in two lines, «C 57r, O 114v,» and «N 26v.», the letters raised; no folio of M is given. Here as left margin notes.",
  "The table: rules and heads as on pp. 138-139 (JP2 0170-0171), with «Pagina numerorum (O)1) Catar eladed (C)» over the four columns of the arguments, divided below a rule into «Augmentum proprietatum omnium2)» and «Dimlnutio proprietatum omnium2)» in smaller type; «Sublimatio ascensus Saturni examinata3)»; «Examinatio ipsius elheza4)» and «Examinatio centri», each hyphenated «Exami-natio»; «Statio prima Saturni»; «Latitudo Saturni accedentis» over «prima» and «secunda» in smaller bold type. Here each head is set from the top, one printed line to a line (L04-L08), the cells separated by our «|»; a head followed by empty cells spans their columns. L09, the units, in bold.",
  "L10-L39. Checked as on p. 138 (JP2 0170): the Latitudo secunda is 5° · sin x to within 0.7″ in every row, 5° 0′ 0″ at 90°. The Sublimatio is 8s 4° 55′ less half the Examinatio argumenti of the same row, a half minute counted as a whole, in every row except 81°-85° (L30-L34): there the Sublimatio, 8s 2° 9′, 8s 2° 8′, 8s 2° 8′, 8s 2° 7′ and 8s 2° 7′, answers to an Examinatio argumenti of 5° 32′ or 5° 33′, 5° 34′ or 5° 35′, 5° 34′ or 5° 35′, 5° 36′ or 5° 37′ and 5° 36′ or 5° 37′, where the table has 5° 35′, 5° 36′, 5° 37′, 5° 38′ and 5° 38′. With the values that the Sublimatio requires, the Examinatio argumenti rises steadily by 1′ or 2′ a degree from 5° 31′ at 80°, where the table steps from 5° 31′ to 5° 35′ at 81°.",
  "Notes a and b. At 85° the edition prints 5° 38′, where C, O and N have 5° 37′: the 37 of the manuscripts agrees with the Sublimatio of the row, 8s 2° 7′, the 38 of the edition does not. At 86° both the edition's 39 and the manuscripts' 38 agree with the Sublimatio, 8s 2° 6′. Without the readings of notes a and b the column falls from 5° 38′ at 84° to 5° 37′ at 85°; by the Sublimatio the excess lies in the values of 81°-84°.",
  "L10-L39, compared with the equation of the epicycle of p. 138 (r = 5.95): the Examinatio argumenti is 1.0′ to 2.6′ larger from 61° to 80° and from 85° to 90°, and 3.5′ to 4.5′ larger from 81° to 84°. The figures that Suter compares at 70° (p. 50, JP2 0082, L20), Sublimatio 242° 20′, Examinatio centri 8° 1′ and Examinatio argumenti 5° 10′, are those of L19.",
  "L10-L39, the other columns, compared from row to row: the Examinatio centri increases by 3′ and 4′ in turn from 61° to 74°, then by 2′ or 1′ a degree, to 8° 36′ at 90°, the greatest equation (8° 36′ of the comparison on p. 139, JP2 0171). The Statio prima increases from 3s 23° 29′ to 3s 24° 11′ by 1′ to 3′ a degree, by 3′ from 74° to 75°. The Latitudo prima is 1° 21′ to 68°, 1° 20′ to 76°, 1° 19′ to 84° and 1° 18′ from 85° on.",
  "Notes on the readings. Checked: the editor's readings agree with the rows around them where the variants depart: the Examinatio centri 7° 54′ and 7° 58′ at 68° and 69° (notes c, against C's 8) and 8° 33′ at 87° (note d); the Statio prima 3s 23° 41′ at 70° (note e) and 3s 24° 8′ at 87° (note f); the Latitudo prima in notes g and h; the Latitudo secunda 5° 0′ 0″ at 90° (note i, N, against the 4 of C and O) and 4° 43′ 39″ at 71° (note k, 4° 43′ 39.3″ computed).",
  "N03: the dash after «37 CON» is shorter than the others, 30 px against 41-43 px, and is transcribed as an en dash. Italics: «om.» and «ras.». N01-N02: the ellipses «. . . .» of the lemmata as printed. L06, «Dimlnutio»: see the critical note."
 ],
